{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Baumeister1969-Filter2-oneM",
  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Radiant power flow and absorptance in thin films,\" Appl. Opt. 8, 423-436 (1969). doi:10.1364/AO.8.000423 - Table VI, p. 430 (Filter 2 = air (LH)^5 1.74B M stack 2; Stack 2 = 1.77L B (LH)^4 quartz); Figs. 12, 14, 16",
  "Notes": "One-M UV bandpass filter with a single 250 A Al film at 2536 A, matching stack 2 (MgO/cryolite quarter-wave stack)\nSpecification: Peak transmittance T_max at lambda0 = 2536 A (R = 0 at lambda0), matching-stack admittance ~ Y_max = 0.96 + j3.35.\nReported: p. 431-432: T_max of the 250 A Al film = 0.739 at Y_max = 0.96 + j3.35; filter peak transmittance very nearly this value with zero reflectance at lambda0. Fig. 16 (abscissa lambda0/lambda 0.7-1.3): peak T ~0.73 at 1.0 with R ~0; passband wider than filter 1 (Fig. 14). Check computed here: T = 0.737, R = 0.002 at 253.6 nm; peak T = 0.738 at 253.5 nm; FWHM ~11.7 nm; stack 2 alone on quartz has admittance 0.91 + j3.39.\nNotes: Normal incidence assumed (no angle given). The notation is read left to right from the incident medium (air) to the substrate (quartz, n = 1.47), so layer 1 is next to air. All dielectric layers are quarter-wave optical thickness (QWOT) at lambda0 = 2536 A = 253.6 nm with constant indices; the Al film is a physical thickness with the nondispersive n - jk = 0.20 - j2.80 at 2536 A (Macleod sign convention; k = +2.80 here). B = thorium oxyfluoride n = 1.55, L = cryolite n = 1.36, H = MgO n = 1.80 (Table VI footnote). Expanded: (LH)^5 = 10 layers, 1.74B, M, then stack 2 = 1.77L, B, (LH)^4: 22 layers. Reproduces the paper's numbers.\nLayers from Table VI, p. 430 (Filter 2 = air (LH)^5 1.74B M stack 2; Stack 2 = 1.77L B (LH)^4 quartz); Figs. 12, 14, 16; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours T 0.737, R 0.002 at 253.6 nm; paper T 0.739 with R = 0 at 253.6 nm.",
